  "question": {
    "text": "What is the correct increasing order of Bronsted bases?"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "$\\mathrm{ClO}_{4}^{-}<\\mathrm{ClO}_{3}^{-}<\\mathrm{ClO}_{2}^{-}<\\mathrm{ClO}^{-}$",
      "is_correct": true
    },
    {
      "text": "$\\mathrm{ClO}_{4}^{-}>\\mathrm{ClO}_{3}^{-}>\\mathrm{ClO}_{2}^{-}>\\mathrm{ClO}^{-}$",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "$\\mathrm{ClO}_{3}^{-}<\\mathrm{ClO}_{4}^{-}<\\mathrm{ClO}_{2}^{-}<\\mathrm{ClO}^{-}$",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "$\\mathrm{ClO}^{-}>\\mathrm{ClO}_{3}^{-}>\\mathrm{ClO}_{2}^{-}<\\mathrm{ClO}_{4}^{-}$",
      "is_correct": false
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "A"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "$\\mathrm{ClO}_{4}^{-}<\\mathrm{ClO}_{3}^{-}<\\mathrm{ClO}_{2}^{-}<\\mathrm{ClO}^{-}$ is the correct\r\n\r\nincreasing order of Bronsted base. With increase in the number of oxygen atoms in the conjugate bases, the delocalisation of the $\\pi$ bond becomes more and more extended. This results in decrease in the electron density. Consequently basicity also decreases."
  }
